WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.730
Analysts must evaluate both sources and the information those sources provide.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.960
Source reliability asks whether the source has relevant access, a trustworthy method,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.660
a history of accurate reporting,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.480
or an understood limitation.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.330
Information credibility asks whether a specific claim is plausible, timely, consistent,

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.200
corroborated,

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.040
and relevant to the intelligence question.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.720
These are related, but they are not the same.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.600
A reliable source can still be wrong.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.210
A vendor advisory may be carefully written but incomplete when first published.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:30.195
An internal dashboard may normally be trustworthy but affected by a logging outage.

00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:34.290
A knowledgeable employee may remember most details accurately but be uncertain about

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.080
timing.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.200
Treating a reliable source as automatically correct can lead to overconfidence.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.720
An unreliable or untested source can still provide a useful lead.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.760
A single unconfirmed report, a rumor,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.490
or an unusual telemetry point may not support a strong judgment by itself,

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.710
but it may tell the analyst what to check next.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.025
The correct response is not to accept or reject everything immediately.

00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:56.250
The correct response is to label the limitation, seek corroboration,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.520
and avoid building a firm conclusion on weak evidence.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:00.635
Credibility depends on the claim.

00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:02.350
Is it consistent with other evidence?

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.680
Is the timing plausible?

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.340
Does the source have access to know this?

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.595
Is there another explanation?

00:01:06.595 --> 00:01:08.475
Is the information current enough to matter?

00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:10.120
Does the claim use clear definitions?

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.530
Does it come from a system or process with known gaps?

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.475
These questions help analysts avoid treating all information as equal.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:19.520
Corroboration improves confidence, especially when it comes from independent sources.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.340
If an incident note, log summary, endpoint telemetry,

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.760
and change record all point to the same sequence,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:25.960
confidence may increase.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.170
If all sources repeat the same original claim,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.080
confidence may not increase as much as it appears.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.920
Analysts should distinguish independent confirmation from repetition.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.370
Documenting source limits is part of professional analysis.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.490
A useful note might say that the source is timely but indirect,

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.800
that the log source did not cover all systems,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.965
that the sample size is small, or that the claim is plausible but uncorroborated.

00:01:45.965 --> 00:01:49.595
These labels help decision makers understand how much weight to place on the evidence and

00:01:49.595 --> 00:01:50.870
help future reviewers update

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.080
the assessment when better information arrives.
